  "text": "��\n.createSequentialGroup}\nLet’s start by understanding the modified Pascal’s triangle and the definition of \\( a_n \\).\n\n1. **Original Pascal’s Triangle**: The entries in the \\( n \\)-th row of Pascal’s triangle are given by the binomial coefficients:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{n}{0}, \\binom{n}{1}, \\binom{n}{2}, \\ldots, \\binom{n}{n}\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Row Reversal**: The row reversal involves reflecting the \\( n \\)-th row across the vertical axis of symmetry. This means that the \\( k \\)-th entry in the row reversal is the negative of the \\( (n - k + 1) \\)-th entry in the original row.\n\n3. **Modified Entries**: The entries in the \\( n \\)-th row of the modified triangle are the differences between the original entries and their row reversal counterparts. Specifically, the \\( k \\)-th entry in the \\( n \\)-th row of the modified triangle is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Modified entry}_k = \\binom{n}{k} - (-1)^k \\binom{n}{n - k + 1}\n   \\]\n   \n   Simplifying further:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Modified entry}_k = \\binom{n}{k} + (-1)^k \\binom{n}{k}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Modified entry}_k = \\binom{n}{k} \\left(1 + (-1)^k\\right)\n   \\]\n   \n   Since \\( 1 + (-1)^k \\) equals 2 when \\( k \\) is odd and 0 when \\( k \\) is even, we can rewrite the modified entries as:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Modified entry}_k = \\begin{cases}\n   2 \\binom{n}{k} & \\text{if } k \\text{ is odd}, \\\\\n   0 & \\text{if } k \\text{ is even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   \n4. **Sum of Absolute Values \\( a_n \\)**: The sum of the absolute values of the entries in the \\( n \\)-th row of the modified triangle is simply the sum of the modified entries:\n   \\[\n   a_n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\left| \\text{Modified entry}_k \\right|\n   \\]\n   From the above, we see that for even \\( n \\), the sum is zero, and for odd \\( n \\), it is:\n   \\[\n   a_n = \\sum_{k=0}^{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor} 2 \\binom{n}{k} = 2 \\sum_{k=0}^{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor} \\binom{n}{k}\n   \\]\n   \n5. **Binomial Sum**: We know that the sum of the binomial coefficients in any row of Pascal’s triangle is \\( 2^n \\), and the sum of the first half of the binomial coefficients is \\( \\binom{2^{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor}}{2^{\\lfloor (n-1)/2 \\rfloor}} \\). However, in our case, the sum is entire half of the coefficients, which is \\( 2^{n-1} \\).\n\n6. **Final Expression for \\( a_n \\)**: Since \\( a_n \\) is the sum of the entire half of the binomial coefficients, we have:\n   \\[\n   a_n = 2^n\n   \\]\n   \n7. **Logarithmic Expression**: We need to express \\( \\frac{\\log_{10} a_n}{\\log_{10} 2} \\) in terms of \\( n \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\log_{10} a_n}{\\log_{10} 2} = \\frac{\\log_{10} (2^n)}{\\log_{10} 2} = \\frac{n \\log_{10} 2}{\\log_{10} 2} = n\n   \\]\n   \nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{n}\n\\]\n_RGBA{0,0,0,0}\n_RGBA{255,255,255,255}\n_RGBA{0,0,255,255}",
